>The band and setting are iron.

I'm following a trend: Tiger eye, jade, agate, topaz, ruby, diamond and
emerald rings are "gemstone", and none of them are "iron", so therefore
pearl must not be "iron" either.

Also, the "opal" (white gem) and "black opal" (black gem) objects are
"gemstone."

>Is a spear wood or iron?
>It's both, but what should it be in the game?

The arrow is iron because the important aspect of the arrow is the
head. A "thoroughly burnt" arrow has a negligible flight penalty (to my
knowledge), but a "thoroughly rusted" arrow should have a damage
penalty.

But, what's the most important part of a wand? (Obvious answer: the end
that you point away from you.)

What I _don't_ know is why a "forked" wand is considered wood, whereas
the other shaped wands (curved, short) are iron.



